1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:01,093
qwentensor.

2
00:00:01,093 --> 00:00:06,954
Write a small PyTorch network, quantize it to five bit integers, and run it inside Qwen three

3
00:00:06,954 --> 00:00:09,979
point five, with no GPU kernels and no training.

4
00:00:09,979 --> 00:00:11,933
The program is just the prompt.

5
00:00:12,930 --> 00:00:17,059
The qwenTPU project showed that Qwen's layers can hold an integer compute array.

6
00:00:17,059 --> 00:00:21,549
But that machine was fixed: one matrix vector product, or one hard coded chain of them.

7
00:00:21,549 --> 00:00:26,400
qwentensor asks whether the program itself can live in the prompt, so that swapping the prompt

8
00:00:26,400 --> 00:00:27,329
swaps the network.

9
00:00:28,330 --> 00:00:33,424
A torch module goes through the torch f x frontend into a float graph, then post training

10
00:00:33,424 --> 00:00:38,116
quantization, with optional quantization aware fine tuning, into an integer graph.

11
00:00:38,116 --> 00:00:43,439
That graph has a bit exact numpy golden, and an executor that writes the schedule, successors

12
00:00:43,439 --> 00:00:45,785
masks and shifts as tokens in the prompt.

13
00:00:45,785 --> 00:00:48,189
The host then only runs argmax and append.

14
00:00:49,190 --> 00:00:53,086
In practice, you call qt dot compile on a module with calibration data.

15
00:00:53,086 --> 00:00:58,189
The result runs a batch, and asserts that what the machine emitted equals the numpy reference

16
00:00:58,189 --> 00:00:58,847
bit for bit.

17
00:00:59,850 --> 00:01:06,206
On real Qwen three point five models on an A100, the digits network ran exact 120 times out of

18
00:01:06,206 --> 00:01:11,075
120, across the zero point eight, two and four billion parameter models.

19
00:01:11,075 --> 00:01:14,117
Every emitted token matched the numpy golden.

20
00:01:15,120 --> 00:01:21,485
A second test compiled one machine and ran four different programs on it, six inputs each

21
00:01:21,485 --> 00:01:23,273
changing only the prompt.

22
00:01:23,273 --> 00:01:29,709
That is 24 of 24 exact on the zero point eight B model, and the same on the two and four B

23
00:01:29,709 --> 00:01:30,209
models.

24
00:01:30,209 --> 00:01:34,000
Only the hidden size changes, from 3,072 up to 4,608.

25
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:37,816
Networks larger than one machine run in pages.

26
00:01:37,816 --> 00:01:43,510
Each page is a fresh prompt, and the host copies integer outputs from one page into the next.

27
00:01:43,510 --> 00:01:48,959
The deep, residual and convolutional networks ran this way, up to 1,320 page runs for the

28
00:01:48,959 --> 00:01:53,611
largest convolutional network, with 0 mismatches across all 3,454 page runs.

29
00:01:54,610 --> 00:02:00,945
Throughput on the zero point eight B model: with CUDA graphs a token takes 3.25 milliseconds

30
00:02:00,945 --> 00:02:02,460
against 13.14 eagerly.

31
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:07,143
That is 4.0 faster per emitted token, or 3.78 inferences per second.

32
00:02:08,140 --> 00:02:08,916
Honest limits.

33
00:02:08,916 --> 00:02:14,071
One machine holds at most one hundred twenty eight ops and one hundred twenty six columns, so

34
00:02:14,071 --> 00:02:18,560
larger networks run in pages, and between pages the host carries the activations.

35
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:21,110
There are no loops or data dependent branches.

36
00:02:21,110 --> 00:02:25,710
And the paged and wide networks were verified on the zero point eight B model only.

37
00:02:26,710 --> 00:02:31,312
The code, the A100 logs, and the design documents are in the repo.

